WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.680
How crop improvement works: genes, pollination and hybrid seed.

2
00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:10.660
This is the video for module 1 of Agricultural Hybridization and Crop Improvement.

3
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:19.230
What you see in a plant comes from its genes plus its environment: soil, rain, weeds, pests and fertiliser.

4
00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.190
Only the genetic part is passed on in seed.

5
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:31.840
Whoever supplies the pollen is the father of the seed. So pollination decides how easy a variety is to keep pure.

6
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.660
You will learn why saved hybrid seed disappoints, and how to set a clear goal.

7
00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:50.960
Genes come in pairs, one from each parent. For example, Ngozi in Enugu State crosses a pure yellow maize with a pure white maize. Yellow is dominant, meaning it hides white.

8
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:59.440
Every seed in the first generation, called F1, gets one yellow and one white copy. All show yellow grain.

9
00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:04.550
She plants the F1 seed and lets the plants pollinate each other.

10
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.940
In the second generation, three of every four combinations carry yellow.

11
00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:21.430
On a cob of 400 kernels, she expects about 300 yellow and 100 white. That splitting is why saved hybrid seed does not stay true.

12
00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:32.110
In self-pollinated crops like rice, cowpea and groundnut, pollen usually comes from the same flower. Saved seed stays close to its parent.

13
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:42.070
In maize, the tassel sheds pollen and the wind carries it to the silks of other plants. Pollen from a neighbour's field can change your next crop.

14
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:55.800
A hybrid is the F1 of two chosen inbred parents. But saved hybrid seed becomes an F2, with uneven plants and much less vigour. So buy fresh hybrid seed every season.

15
00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:07.950
An improved open-pollinated variety, or OPV, comes from a research institute. You can save its seed for a few seasons. It depends on your cash, inputs and market.

16
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:19.220
For example, Halima farms 1 hectare of maize near Zaria. She compares a hybrid with an improved OPV, both sown at 20 kg per hectare.

17
00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:30.240
Hybrid seed at about 5,000 naira per kg costs 100,000 naira every season, so 300,000 naira over three seasons.

18
00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:39.350
Certified OPV seed at about 2,500 naira per kg costs 50,000 naira in season 1.

19
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:49.450
Then she keeps back 20 kg of her own grain each season, worth about 14,000 naira, so 28,000 naira for two seasons.

20
00:02:50.700 --> 00:03:01.540
The hybrid costs about 222,000 naira more in seed. It is worth it only if its extra harvest, after extra fertiliser, is worth more than that.

21
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:09.610
Step 1. Name the problem. For example, my maize dries before the cobs fill when the rains stop early.

22
00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:17.010
Step 2. Find the likely cause. Is it the variety, planting date, soil, weeds or a pest?

23
00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:24.680
Step 3. If the variety is part of it, name the trait, here earlier maturity or drought tolerance.

24
00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.090
Step 4. Add your conditions and affordable inputs.

25
00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.580
Step 5. Add the market, like the grain colour your buyers pay for.

26
00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:45.170
A new variety cannot fix late weeding or very poor soil. And never change seed and management at the same time, or you cannot know which helped.

27
00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:53.060
For example, Blessing farms in Niger State. Her must-haves are white grain and maturity within her season.

28
00:03:54.310 --> 00:04:01.330
She weights her nice-to-have traits: drought tolerance 4, yield potential 3, streak virus resistance 2,

29
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.520
and seed price 1. The weights add up to 10.

30
00:04:06.770 --> 00:04:16.760
She scores each variety from 1 to 5 and multiplies by the weights. Variety B scores 41, A scores 39 and C scores 29.

31
00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:23.920
She drops C. B and A go into a trial beside her usual seed. Her own trial decides.

32
00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:31.310
Before the season, write your goal and must-have traits. Choose even land with the same soil and slope.

33
00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:39.720
Set aside cash for seed and the fertiliser it needs. Get a notebook or sheet ready, and know your ADP extension agent.

34
00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:57.720
To remember. Only genes pass on in seed. A uniform F1 splits in the next generation, so buy fresh hybrid seed each season. Maize crosses easily, so it needs isolation. And start from a written goal, not a colourful seed bag.

35
00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:11.410
Now map pollination for every crop you plant. Check hybrid and OPV prices with one agro-dealer and redo the three-season sum. Then write your variety profile with weighted scores.

36
00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:13.700
Continue with the lessons below.
